(a) Generally. The nature of law enforcement requires Department employees to have the ability to work irregular duty schedules which are subject to change in meeting deployment needs. Additionally, it is necessary that an employee have adequate rest to be alert during his tour of duty. For these reasons, and because certain occupations inherently conflict with an employee's primary responsibility to the Department, the Department may impose conditions on outside employment or may prohibit it altogether. Determination of the degree of limitation will be based upon the interest of the Department in furthering professionalization, protection of the reputation of the employee and the Department, and ensuring that the Department receives full and faithful service in return for employment.
(b) Policy. Members of the City Police Department may engage in off-duty employment subject to the following limitations:
(1) Such employment shall not interfere with the member's employment with the Department.
(2) Members shall submit a written request for off-duty employment to the Chief, whose approval must be granted prior to engaging in such employment.
(3) Members shall not engage in any employment or business involving:
a. The sale or distribution of alcoholic beverages;
b. Bail bond agencies;
c. Private guard agencies;
d. Collection agencies;
e. Attorneys.
(4) Approval of other employment may be denied where it appears to the Chief that the outside employment might:
a. Render the member unavailable during an emergency;
b. Physically or mentally exhaust the member to the point that his performance may be affected;
c. Require that any special consideration be given to scheduling of the member's regular duty hours;
d. Bring the Department or the member into disrepute or impair the operation or efficiency of the Department or member;
e. Involve more than 20 hours of work each week.
(5) No employee will engage in any type of outside employment which may raise the question of honesty, respect, dignity, or loyalty to the Department or to the laws which the employee is to uphold.
(6) Any employee engaged in outside employment will be expected and required to conduct in a manner becoming of an officer. Any conduct during outside employment which if it occurred during duty status hours, would result in disciplinary action, could result in termination of the outside employment by the employee involved.
(7) The Chief of Police reserves the right to require any employee to discontinue any outside employment if, in his opinion, the outside employment is unsatisfactory. Failure to comply with the Chief's request will result in disciplinary action.
(Code 2003, § 3-1025; Mo. of 3-1-1988)

